
Creating a bew bhyve service instance "ootest"
with config file under /tank/vm/ootest/

Create a directory for this VM, eg. /tank/zone/ootest
Create some virtual disks (typicall under this location)
eg. zfs create -V 40G tank/zone/ootest/disk0

First create a vnic for the new VM, eg
 dladm create-vnic -l igb0 bhyve0

Craft a bhyve(8) command using that vnic=bhyve0
then test/adjust until you're happy with it.
Save your bhyve invocation to a script, eg run-ootest.sh

Create a "config" file for bhyve based on the script, eg.
 bhyve ...normal...options... -o config.dump=true
and redirect the output to "config".
The output file will contain config.dump=true
which you should remove with an editor.

Save the "config" file to the directory the VM will use.
You can check it by running: bhyve -k config

Now setup a service instance for this VM:

Create an instance like this:
 name=ootest
 svccfg -s svc:/application/bhyve add $name
 svccfg -s svc:/application/bhyve:$name addpg vm application
 svccfg -s svc:/application/bhyve:$name setprop vm/inst_root=/tank/zone/$name

Look at the properties:
 svcprop -p vm application/bhyve:$name

If your config file is OK, you can now just:
svcadm enable application/bhyve:$name

Things to watch out for:
Each VM needs its own vnic (bhyve0, bhyve1, ...)
Eavh VM using rfb needs a listneing port (5900, 59001, ...)
Each VM using serial ports needs a unique UNIX sock name.
